      overviewText: 'The palette is a decision about who owns light. Readable midtones and ink silhouettes carry the city; sodium amber belongs to practical fixtures; cyan belongs to Hidzu Corporation devices and nothing else; crimson appears only where danger or restriction is real. No broad neon washes, no fantasy skyline — institutional dystopia is served by the city looking normal. The architecture is one coherent identity: a licensed Neo Tokyo kit recomposed into a single four-block master scene, every building placed with provenance, so the district holds together like a place instead of a collage.',
      principleTitle: 'The camera serves play',
      principleBody: 'Normal play is a close, street-first 2:1 isometric frame with the protagonist in the lower-center lead — near enough to read faces, doors, and device lights. Maximum zoom-out lands on one deliberately composed four-block overview. Nothing between exists as a default: framing is a designed range, not a slider left to chance.',
      flowTitle: 'From authored city to live frame',
      flowSteps: [
        'Compose the full four-block relationship in one master scene, mission semantics intact.',
        'Prove the frame at street level and at overview from the same geometry.',
        'Reconcile visible footprints with collision, occlusion, entrances, and anchors before anything ships.',
        'Place actors as separate grounded figures: 64×96, eight facings, idle, move, and interact — about fifteen percent larger than strict architectural scale, because readable people outrank perfect perspective.',
        'Carry each face from creation through HUD to dialogue portrait as one continuous identity.',
        'Light by schedule: dusk, wind-down, curfew — practical sources first, and actors pick up a subtle amber or cyan cast from the lit regions they stand in. The tint is presentation only; it never touches detection or play.',
      ],
      exampleText: 'At street scale: a concrete wall in cold midtone, a sodium lamp marking the service door, one cyan pinpoint that means a camera is alive. At overview: the same blocks, loops, and thresholds, still legible, with people still people rather than icons. If a frame could be mistaken for a generic neon game, the frame is wrong — the reference is a working city at night, photographed by someone who knows what to fear in it.',
